{
 "hash": "0c0abb01130f8c",
 "text": "The second our hands touched the ball, a jolt of lightning shot straight between us. My fingers tingled. KD blinked and shook his hand out. The next morning, I stepped onto the court. I dribbled once—and the ball moved like it was on a string. I pulled up from mid-range. Swish. Again. Swish. I drove to the rim, jumped, and dunked. I'd never even touched the net before. My pull-up middy was 100%. I couldn't miss. I destroyed school tryouts. Someone filmed me dropping 40 points, and by the end of the month, I was all over the news. Even with the magic, KD's words stayed in my head. \"You need to work hard, kid.\" So I didn't get lazy. For months, I woke up at 5 AM. Rain, snow, didn't matter. Five hundred pull-up middies before school, every single day. Meanwhile, on TV, something was wrong. Kevin Durant was missing everything. Airballs. Bricks. Turnovers. The media said his career was over. I stared at my hands and realized the truth. I didn't just get his talent. I took it. Because I was the \"viral miracle kid,\" the Thunder invited me to suit up and play alongside KD. In the tunnel before the fourth quarter, KD sat on the bench with his head down. I walked over and held out my signed basketball. I'm sorry. This belongs to you. When he grabbed it, the lightning snapped right back. My arms felt heavy. The magic was gone. I was just me again. Five seconds left. Down by one. KD was instantly back—draining threes, carrying the team. So on the final play, the other team panicked and sent three defenders straight at him. Coach looked down the bench and pointed at me.",
 "voice": "leo",
 "lead": 0,
 "end": 100.588,
 "dur": 100.623673,
